  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 102 PM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nA welcomed pattern change will move through by mid-week due to\nthe remnants of Hurricane Simon getting ingested into an upper-\nlevel wave. This system will move through the Plains early this\nweek and bring us a chance by early Wednesday morning. Rain is \nforecast to start near sunrise and will continue intermittently \nthrough Thursday evening as a couple of cold fronts move through\nthe area. \n\nSevere Threat?: Our PWAT values will be near record for this \nevent from 2.0-2.5\" which is normally near 1.67\" for this time \nof year. This means these rain showers will be efficient \nrainfall producers which could lead to nuisance flooding if rain\nshowers train over the same area. There is a chance for a \nstrong thunderstorm or two on Wednesday by the late \nmorning/early afternoon hours. Any strong to severe storms that\noccur will be over a very localized area and will only last for\na short amount of time. Right now, SPC has a Marginal Risk \n(1/5) for severe weather issued for Wednesday. This area goes as\nfar west as Pittsburg, KS, down to the MO/AR border, as far \nnorth as Stockton/Lebanon and as far east as Mountain Grove. \nThere will be a small window of time where 0-6km bulk shear will\nbe near 35 kts and MUCAPE of ~2500 J/kg in an uncapped \nenvironment just ahead of the cold front. Though, this favorable\nenvironment won't last long as the environment quickly becomes \nstable as the first front moves through. It's important to note \nthat this severe risk is highly conditional due to how \noverworked the atmosphere will be from the rain that will have \noccurred earlier in the morning. If the atmosphere can't \nrecover, we'll just see rain and miss out on the strong/severe \nstorms. For this reason, we are not completely sold on the idea \nthat we'll have severe weather on Wednesday. \n\nRainfall Amounts: As for rainfall totals, the amounts continue \nto decrease with every forecast update. Now, the range is 0.5-3\"\nacross the area. There is a sharp gradient of rainfall amounts \nwith the lower amounts (0.5-1.5\") occurring southeast of I-44 \nand the higher amounts (1.75-3\") northwest of the interstate. \nUnfortunately, this sharp cut-off in rainfall amounts may occur \nover Greene county.\n\nTemperatures: As for temperatures for the rest of the week, \nhighs will be in the upper 70s to mid 80s for Wednesday and \nThursday. By Friday, we'll see a change in temperatures with \nhighs only in the lower 60s and lows getting down into the mid \n30s to upper 40s. It'll feel chilly compared to the mid 90s \nwe'll be experiencing earlier this week. High pressure moves in \nthis weekend with another day of perfect fall weather on \nSaturday with highs in the 60s and lows in the 40s and Sunday a \nbit warmer with highs in the 70s and lows in the upper 40s and \nlower 50s.",
